Tier 1: the indispensable research nucleus

These should become the initial “scholar nodes” in Hermes.

Ficino and Renaissance Platonism
Anna Corrias

Probably the single most valuable living Ficino scholar for your project.

Her work covers:

Ficino’s imagination and memory;
vehicles of the soul;
the daimon or tutelary spirit;
Plotinian vision;
the World Soul;
ensoulment;
human nature in Ficino’s Enneads commentary.

Start with:

The Renaissance of Plotinus: The Soul and Human Nature in Marsilio Ficino’s Commentary on the Enneads
“Imagination and Memory in Marsilio Ficino’s Theory of the Vehicles of the Soul”
“From Daemonic Reason to Daemonic Imagination”
“Plotinus’s Language of Seeing”

Her work directly connects soul, imagination, post-mortem embodiment, vision and the individual daimon. PhilPapers’ Ficino bibliography identifies her vehicle-of-soul article as a central study of Ficino’s imagination and immortality.

Michael J. B. Allen

The foundational Anglophone authority on Ficino’s philosophical Platonism.

Especially relevant:

Ficino’s Plato commentaries;
the Phaedrus, Symposium, Ion and Philebus;
divine madness;
the soul’s ascent;
Platonic theology;
the Renaissance interpretation of ancient myths.

Allen is indispensable for preventing your Ficino from becoming merely an occult or therapeutic figure.

Stephen Gersh

The strongest bridge among:

Plotinus;
Iamblichus;
Proclus;
medieval Platonism;
Ficino.

Read Gersh whenever you need to determine whether an idea is genuinely Plotinian, later Proclean, or Ficino’s own synthesis.

Brian P. Copenhaver

Essential for:

Ficino’s magic;
Proclean and Iamblichean sources;
Three Books on Life;
celestial causality;
the relationship between philosophy, magic and medicine;
Hermetic versus Neoplatonic influence.

The recent overview of fifty years of Ficino studies identifies Copenhaver’s work on Ficino’s use of Proclus, Hermes and Zoroaster as a major advance in the field.

Denis Robichaud

One of the most important current Ficino scholars.

Particularly relevant for:

Ficino’s textual practices;
Plato as a living philosophical persona;
prisca theologia;
ancient theology;
Ficino’s relation to Plotinus and later Platonism;
manuscripts and intellectual networks.

His Plato’s Persona is especially valuable for your interest in texts as living teachers rather than static information.

Jacomien Prins

The key scholar for:

Ficino and music;
cosmic harmony;
musical contemplation;
the Timaeus;
the therapeutic and metaphysical function of sound.

Read her work whenever your project moves from metaphysics into actual Ficinian practices of tuning the soul.

Sokratis-Athanasios Kiosoglou

Directly relevant because of his source-critical work on Ficino’s use of Proclus’ Elements of Physics and Elements of Theology.

He is a particularly useful model for Hermes: identify the source proposition, observe how Ficino modifies it, and track its new argumentative role.

John Finamore

The most important specialist for:

Iamblichus’ psychology;
the vehicle of the soul;
imagination;
descent and embodiment;
De anima;
late-Platonic mediation.

His work gives you the technical machinery behind the Ficino–Corbin subtle-body bridge.

Crystal Addey

Extremely valuable for your project’s practical and epistemological side:

theurgy;
divination;
inspiration;
religious experience;
philosophy as a contemplative way of life;
the relationship between reason and divine signs.

Her Divination and Theurgy in Neoplatonism should be a core bridge text.

Radek Chlup

One of the clearest contemporary interpreters of Proclus.

Read him for:

participation;
symbolic mediation;
unity and multiplicity;
the structure of the Proclean cosmos;
how late-Platonic metaphysics functions as an integrated system.
Edward Butler

Important for:

Proclean henadology;
divine individuals;
polycentric metaphysics;
participation;
theurgy;
the relationship between gods and Forms.

Butler can help you formulate how an angel, god or daimon could be personally distinct without being an isolated substance.

Corbin and Islamic imaginal thought
John V. Garner

Probably the most important emerging academic interpreter of Corbin for your project.

His work brings Corbin into conversation with:

phenomenology;
Sartre;
Merleau-Ponty;
imagination;
perceptual orientation;
disclosure of worlds.

His article explicitly positions Corbin’s creative imagination within French phenomenology.

Lydia Idinopulos

Her 2026 thesis, Henry Corbin’s Imaginal and the Space of the Fictional, is almost perfectly aligned with Imaginarium.

It investigates:

reading as a journey;
fictional and imaginal space;
phenomenology;
hermeneutics;
transformation through texts.

This is one of the clearest scholarly formulations of your idea that entering a text’s world may constitute a genuine mode of cognition.

Elliot R. Wolfson

A major scholar of mysticism, language and phenomenology whose recent Corbin work examines:

imaginal time;
veil and disclosure;
linear and cyclical temporality;
mystical imagination;
hermeneutics.

His approach is philosophically rigorous and comparatively informed.

Tom Cheetham

Not the most historically detached Corbin scholar, but one of the most sustained and creative interpreters of Corbin in English.

Useful for:

imaginal ecology;
poetic knowledge;
angelology;
the loss of the world-soul;
Corbin and Hillman;
imagination as a mode of life.

Treat Cheetham as a constructive extension of Corbin, not simply neutral commentary.

Daryush Shayegan

Crucial for:

Corbin’s Iranian context;
comparative philosophy;
spiritual geography;
cultural consciousness;
East–West interpretation;
the intellectual world around Corbin in Iran.
Christian Jambet

One of the strongest French successors to Corbin in the philosophical study of Shiʿism and Islamic philosophy.

Important themes:

Mullā Ṣadrā;
resurrection;
spiritual body;
being and act;
the Imam;
esoteric Shiʿite philosophy.

Jambet is less romantic and often more philosophically exact than popular Corbin interpreters.

Pierre Lory

Central for:

Islamic esotericism;
Ibn ʿArabī;
letter mysticism;
alchemy;
occult sciences;
language and divine manifestation.

He is especially relevant to your interest in calligraphy, letters, image and creation.

William Chittick

The indispensable systematic interpreter of Ibn ʿArabī in English.

His work establishes the architecture of:

imagination;
divine self-disclosure;
the Names;
barzakh;
cosmology;
the heart;
religious diversity.

His SEP treatment stresses Ibn ʿArabī’s idea that knowledge must be realised as an actualisation of the soul rather than accepted through imitation.

James Winston Morris

Vital for Ibn ʿArabī as a practical hermeneutic and spiritual pedagogy, especially through the Meccan Revelations.

Morris is often better than purely systematic interpreters at explaining how Ibn ʿArabī’s texts are designed to transform the reader.

Salman Bashier

Read him for:

barzakh;
limit and mediation;
Ibn ʿArabī and philosophy;
the relation between unity and difference;
Islamic philosophical narratives.

His work on barzakh gives you one of the strongest formal bridges to Ficino’s mediating soul and Corbin’s intermediate imaginal world.

Roxanne Marcotte

One of the most reliable Suhrawardī specialists.

Her research covers:

illuminationist epistemology;
imagination;
soul;
philosophical psychology;
innateness;
eschatology;
the later Illuminationist tradition.

She authored the major contemporary SEP overview of Suhrawardī, whose bibliography maps the specialist field.

John Walbridge

Indispensable for Suhrawardī’s relationship to:

Plato;
Greek philosophy;
ancient wisdom;
illumination;
the later Ishrāqī tradition;
Quṭb al-Dīn Shīrāzī.

His major studies include The Leaven of the Ancients, The Wisdom of the Mystic East and The Science of Mystic Lights.

Mehdi Aminrazavi

Important for:

Suhrawardī;
the School of Illumination;
Persian philosophical traditions;
mystical epistemology;
comparative wisdom.
Hossein Ziai

A major technical scholar of Suhrawardī’s logic and Illuminationist philosophy.

He is essential for preventing a purely visionary reading of Suhrawardī. Ziai shows the rigorous logical and epistemological framework underlying illumination.

Hermann Landolt

Important for:

Persian Sufism;
Islamic Neoplatonism;
esotericism;
spiritual interpretation;
Ismaili and Sufi traditions.
Mohammad Ali Amir-Moezzi

A leading scholar of early Shiʿism and Imamology.

Essential for testing Corbin’s accounts of:

the Imam;
esoteric revelation;
divine guidance;
light;
visionary knowledge;
Shiʿite scripture and tradition.
Sajjad Rizvi

Important for later Islamic philosophy, especially:

Mullā Ṣadrā;
Shiʿite thought;
being and knowledge;
post-Avicennan philosophy;
contemporary reception.
Nader El-Bizri

Highly relevant to the Avicenna–Heidegger–phenomenology bridge.

His work deals with:

Avicenna;
phenomenology;
ontology;
architecture and space;
Heidegger;
Islamic philosophy.
Peter Adamson

A major organiser of the wider history of Islamic philosophy.

Less Corbinian, but indispensable for:

historical discipline;
Avicenna;
post-Avicennan philosophy;
philosophy in the Islamic world;
correcting simplistic lineage claims.
Ñāṇananda and Buddhist phenomenology
Bhikkhunī Dhammadinnā

The most directly relevant contemporary defender and interpreter of Ñāṇananda’s Concept and Reality.

Her reply to Stephen Evans should be attached to the same debate node as Evans’ criticism.

Stephen A. Evans

Important because he provides one of the few sustained scholarly criticisms of Ñāṇananda’s interpretation of papañca.

He prevents Hermes from treating Ñāṇananda as uncontested Buddhist doctrine.

Bhikkhu Anālayo

Probably the most valuable living textual scholar for checking whether claims about early Buddhist meditation are supported across parallel canons.

Important for:

early Buddhist meditation;
mindfulness;
dependent arising;
emptiness;
consciousness;
comparative sutta research.
Sue Hamilton

Essential for treating early Buddhist teachings as analyses of lived experience rather than lists of metaphysical entities.

Read:

Identity and Experience
Early Buddhism: A New Approach
“Anattā: A Different Approach”

Her work is highly compatible with the phenomenological direction of your project. A broad early-Buddhist research archive lists her work alongside Ñāṇananda, Peter Harvey and Kalupahana as major studies of mind, self and experience.

Peter Harvey

Vital for:

consciousness;
selflessness;
personality;
Nibbāna;
early Buddhist mind-body theory;
Buddhist cosmology.

His The Selfless Mind is particularly valuable because it avoids turning non-self into simple eliminative materialism.

Christian Coseru

One of the strongest bridges between Buddhist philosophy and contemporary phenomenology.

Relevant themes:

intentionality;
perception;
reflexivity;
cognition;
consciousness;
phenomenological method.
Dan Lusthaus

Essential for Yogācāra interpreted as Buddhist phenomenology rather than crude metaphysical idealism.

His work helps extend the Ñāṇananda stream into:

construction of experience;
cognition;
intentionality;
appropriation;
world and object constitution.
Evan Thompson

Important but should be read critically.

Relevant for:

Buddhism and cognitive science;
phenomenology;
enactive cognition;
self;
consciousness;
embodiment;
criticism of Buddhist exceptionalism.

He can connect the project to modern models of enacted worlds without simply endorsing traditional metaphysics.

Francisco Varela

Foundational for:

neurophenomenology;
enaction;
embodied cognition;
Buddhism and cognitive science;
worlds arising through organism–environment relations.
Michel Bitbol

Very relevant to your metaphysical problem:

participatory realism;
quantum theory and experience;
Madhyamaka;
consciousness;
relational ontology.
Jonardon Ganeri

Important for:

self;
attention;
Indian philosophy of mind;
reflexivity;
subjectivity without a substantial soul;
cross-cultural philosophy.
Amber Carpenter

Strong on Buddhist ethics, personhood and philosophical method.

Her work can help distinguish liberation-oriented Buddhist reasoning from merely theoretical deconstruction.

Mark Siderits

Key analytic interpreter of:

reductionism;
conventional truth;
persons;
Madhyamaka;
no-self.

Useful as a sharper, more austere counterpart to Burbea’s creative use of emptiness.

Jay Garfield

Important for:

Madhyamaka;
dependent arising;
conventional reality;
perception;
ethics;
cross-cultural philosophy.
Jan Westerhoff

Especially useful for:

Nāgārjuna;
metaphysical foundations;
emptiness;
conceptual construction;
the limits of ontology.
David Seyfort Ruegg

One of the deepest historical and philological scholars of Madhyamaka, Buddha-nature and Indian Buddhist thought.

Nāgārjuna scholars to include
Joseph Walser
Richard Hayes
Douglas Berger
C. W. Huntington
Tom Tillemans
Georges Dreyfus
Thupten Jinpa

They become important when you test whether Burbea’s “empty fabrication” is a legitimate development of Madhyamaka or a new synthetic practice.

Rupert Gethin

Essential for early Buddhist cosmology, meditation and the structure of the path.

Particularly useful for understanding how devas, meditative worlds and liberation coexist in traditional Buddhist thought.

Richard Gombrich

Important for the formation and historical context of early Buddhist ideas.

Alexander Wynne

Important for reconstructing early Buddhist meditation and its relation to pre-Buddhist contemplative culture.

Tilmann Vetter

Important for historical reconstruction of the earliest Buddhist path and the relation between concentration and insight.

Johannes Bronkhorst

Relevant for the Indian contemplative and philosophical environment in which Buddhism developed.

L. S. Cousins

Highly valuable for Theravāda meditation, Abhidhamma and the historical development of samatha and vipassanā.

David J. Kalupahana

A significant predecessor to your phenomenological Buddhism stream.

Read him for:

Buddhist psychology;
causality;
language;
pragmatism;
anti-essentialism.
Steven Collins

Important for:

personhood;
nirvana;
Pāli literature;
Buddhist imagination;
the relationship between doctrine and narrative worlds.
Matthew MacKenzie

A strong contemporary scholar of:

Buddhist philosophy of mind;
reflexivity;
enaction;
self-awareness;
metaphysics.
Sonam Kachru

Potentially very important for Imaginarium.

His work examines Buddhist philosophy through:

attention;
imagination;
self-formation;
literature;
philosophical exercises;
the texture of lived experience.
Burbea, soulmaking and imaginal practice

The academic literature directly about Burbea remains small. The best “scholar map” therefore includes his intellectual relatives and successors.

Catherine McGee

The most important living practitioner-teacher directly associated with Burbea’s Soulmaking Dharma.

She co-taught advanced soulmaking retreats with Burbea and continues developing the practice. The retreat material explicitly combines emptiness, samatha, mettā, energy body and imaginal practice.

Yahel Avigur

A central current teacher and articulator of Soulmaking Dharma.

Especially relevant for:

imaginal practice;
eros;
emptiness;
sacredness;
pedagogy after Burbea.
Zohar Lavie

Another important teacher within the Burbea lineage, especially around emptiness, ethics and soulmaking.

Nathan Glyde

Relevant for contemporary teaching and interpretation of Burbea’s emptiness practices.

Michael Taft

Not a scholar in the conventional academic sense, but a valuable interviewer and interpreter of advanced meditation, nonduality and emptiness. His interview with Burbea provides a clear account of Seeing That Frees.

Will Franks

An emerging constructive writer on Soulmaking Dharma.

Useful as a map of how Burbea’s system is currently being interpreted rather than as a definitive scholarly authority.

James Hillman

The indispensable precursor for the word and project of soulmaking.

Core themes:

image as primary;
anima mundi;
polytheistic psychology;
beauty;
personification;
rejection of reductive interpretation;
soul as depth rather than substance.
Patricia Berry

Important for imaginal psychology, metaphor and the autonomy of images.

Mary Watkins

Important for:

imaginal dialogues;
personification;
liberation psychology;
active imagination;
multiple worlds of psyche.
Robert Romanyshyn

Relevant for:

imaginal research methods;
embodied hermeneutics;
research as transformative encounter;
reverie;
the unfinished business between researcher and subject.

His work could directly inform how Hermes handles scholarship without pretending to be a detached extraction machine.

Wolfgang Giegerich

A powerful critic of naïve archetypal and imaginal psychology.

Include him as a counterweight to Hillman and Corbin.

David L. Miller

Important for polytheistic imagination, myth and theology.

Thomas Moore

A popular rather than highly technical interpreter, but relevant for the modern diffusion of soulmaking and care of the soul.

Tier 2: crucial bridge scholars
Plato, Plotinus and the transformation of desire
Pierre Hadot

Foundational for understanding ancient philosophy as:

spiritual exercise;
transformation of perception;
a way of life;
contemplation rather than abstract system-building.

He is one of the strongest bridges between Ficino’s philosophy and actual practice.

John M. Dillon

Essential for Middle Platonism and the historical development between Plato and Plotinus.

Dominic O’Meara

Important for Plotinus, political philosophy and the practical dimensions of Neoplatonism.

Lloyd Gerson

A major systematic interpreter of Platonism and Plotinus.

Use him for clarifying the metaphysical commitments of the Platonic stream.

Eyjólfur Emilsson

Important for Plotinus on:

intellect;
perception;
self;
consciousness;
vision.
Sara Rappe

Highly relevant to:

Neoplatonic contemplation;
reading;
language;
ineffability;
the transformative interpretation of texts.
Pauliina Remes

Important for selfhood and subjectivity in Plotinus and later Platonism.

Svetla Slaveva-Griffin

Relevant for Plotinus, number, metaphysical structure and philosophical method.

Kevin Corrigan

A valuable comparative scholar of:

Plato;
Plotinus;
Christian thought;
contemplation;
love;
the soul.
Algis Uždavinys

A major synthetic interpreter of theurgy, Egyptian wisdom and Neoplatonic ritual.

Very relevant, but distinguish his constructive perennialist interpretations from cautious historical scholarship.

Peter Struck

Important for:

divination;
signs;
ancient interpretation;
the philosophical history of inspired knowledge.
Anne Sheppard

Relevant to Neoplatonic theories of imagination, aesthetics and theurgy.

John Dillon and Polymnia Athanassiadi

Athanassiadi is especially valuable for:

late antique religious experience;
dreams;
theurgy;
divine madness;
ritual practice.
